  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 338 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Strong to severe storms with multiple hazards \nare expected today and tonight. \n\nAfternoon weather analysis shows a trough with multiple 700mb \nshortwaves tracking along the axis helping to Initiate showers and \nthunderstorms. While at the surface a weak low pressure system has \nbeen noted over NOVA and south-central MD with a warm front draped \nacross the Eastern Shore and a Lee trough located along the I-95 \nCorridor. Thunderstorm development is already on its way across SE \nVA and NE NC. These storms entering are entering a favorable \nenvironment with 2000-2500 J/kg of MLCape and Bulk-shear of 25-35kt. \nThis environment is suitable;e for strong to severe wind gusts and \nsmall to severe hail with any stronger updraft. This looks to be the \nfirst round of severe weather of the afternoon. Through afternoon \nand into the evening expect storm coverage and the severe to \nincrease especially across the Piedmont and MD Eastern Shore. A \nstrong cold front is expected to approach from this north and will \nhelp initiate numerous thunderstorms. The environment will continue \nto remain primed across the areas as there has been limited \nconvection across the area. Even with dew points slightly mixing \nbehind the Lee trough a moist environment will remain in place \nacross the Piedmont with MLcape values between 1500-2000J/kg and the \nshear is expected to increase as the trough slowly moves south with \nbulk shear values increasing to 30-40kt. The main threat across VA \nand NC through the evening is damaging winds and severe hail. The \ntornado threat remains maximized further north due to lower LCL and \ncloser proximity to the warm front. However, cannot rule a brief \nspin up across the the Northern Neck as it is in closer proximity to \nthe low. Again, in regards to the Tornado threat the highest \nconfidence in a tornado or two is located across the MD Eastern \nShore. A warm front is expected to stall in the vicinity helping to \nenhance the low-level shear and SRH. Recent model guidance is \nshowing 0-1 SRH in the realm of 100-200 m^2/S^2 which is conducive \nfor tornadoes. With these atmospheric components in place a severe \nthunderstorm watch has been issued for VA and NC and a Tornado watch \nhas been issued for MD Eastern Shore. These watches are through 10pm \nas of now and trends in storms and the atm will continue to be \nmonitored through the afternoon and evening. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...  Thunderstorms today and tomorrow will cause heavy \nrainfall potentially leading to localized Flash Flooding. \n\nIn regards to the hydro threat today and tomorrow, given the \nexpected slow frontal progression, concern for several waves of \nlocally heavy rainfall the threat for Flash Flooding continues to \nincrease. Pwats have risen to  2-2.3\" today allowing for heavy rain \npotential to increase along the lee trough and then later this \nevening the expected stalled frontal boundary. Broad forcing for \nascent ahead of the initial shortwave this afternoon is resulting in \nincreasing thunderstorm coverage. The HREF continues to back off  on \nthe probability of 3\"/3hr over the Eastern VA (including Tidewater) \nand Eastern Shore today and tomorrow. However, given the environment \nin place localized flash flooding is a threat especially in the \nurban areas. A Flood watch has not been issued due to some slight \ndisagreement in the models. The front lingers over the region \ntomorrow before pushing toward the coast on Saturday, keeping our \narea downstream of the slow-moving trough axis with rounds of \nconvection persisting Friday and Saturday along coastal areas. As \nmentioned earlier models continue to remain in slight disagreement \non where the frontal boundary stalls on Friday. With the \ndisagreement a Flood watch has not been issued at this time. \nHowever, a Flood watch maybe needed for late Friday into Saturday \nonce location of the stalled boundary is noted. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures Sunday \nthrough Tuesday.\n\nModel ensembles remain in good agreement that the main upper trough\naxis shifts offshore by Sunday, driving the cold front south and\neast of the region. In its wake, surface high pressure builds over\nthe Mid-Atlantic, introducing a drier air mass and seasonable\ntemperatures through early next week.",
